   <text>
      <body>
         <div type="letter">
                <div type="mainBody">
                    <dateline>
                        <name type="geo" key="G_FRA_PARI">Paris</name> <date when="1933-10-12">October the 12-th.1933</date>
                    </dateline>
                    <opener>
                        <salute>Dear Mr Budgen,</salute>
                    </opener>
                    <p>Here are a few points which Mr Joyce wishes me to write to you about.</p>
                    <p>
                        <seg xml:id="seg1" type="note-anchor">NORSE</seg> – you could add the reference to your essay in Exagmination.</p>
                    <p>
                        <seg xml:id="seg2" type="note-anchor">Germany</seg> – Touch up if possible the political discussion in the Consular office – J.J. opinion is of some <choice>
                            <sic>unedeletable</sic>
                            <corr>undeletable</corr>
                        </choice> touch of friendship between English people and Germans. The English look at Germans as their cousin on the Continent who is doing well.</p>
                    <p>
                        <seg xml:id="seg3" type="note-anchor">Wine-vine</seg> – you might say that you have your own vine growing in your garden.</p>
                    <p>Racing – <seg xml:id="seg4" type="note-anchor">allude</seg> to your own <choice>
                            <sic>svheme</sic>
                            <corr>scheme</corr>
                        </choice>.</p>
                    <p>
                        <name type="person" key="gied93">Gideon</name>’s <seg xml:id="seg5" type="note-anchor">address</seg> is 7 Doldertahl strasse</p>
                    <p xml:space="preserve"><name type="person" key="rugg87">Ruggiero</name>                 	call them both up.</p>
                    <closer>
                        <salute>sincerely yours.</salute>
                        <signed>
                            <seg xml:id="seg6" type="note-anchor">
                                <name type="person" key="leon93">Paul Léon</name>
                            </seg>
                        </signed>
                    </closer>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div type="notes">
                <note xml:id="note1" target="#seg1" type="commentary">Also urged by JJ in the letter <rs type="letter" key="L_71712">to Budgen from 14 May 1933</rs>. Though there is passing reference to the Norse influence in <name type="work" key="W_FINNEGANSWAKE">Finnegans Wake</name> (<name type="work">JJMU</name> 312), Budgen did not make reference to his earlier essay (“James Joyce's Work in Progress and Old Norse Poetry”, <name type="work">Our Exagmination Round His Factification for Incamination of Work in Progress</name> [Paris: Shakespeare and Company, <name type="person" key="beac87">Sylvia Beach</name>, 1929], 37-46).</note>
                <note xml:id="note2" target="#seg2" type="commentary">The Germans' “doing well” is mentioned in Budgen’s essay, “Further Recollections of James Joyce” (<name type="work">JJMU</name> 358). The political discussion is in <name type="work">JJMU</name> 154-55.</note>
                <note xml:id="note3" target="#seg3" type="commentary">Not used in <name type="work">JJMU</name>.</note>
                <note xml:id="note4" target="#seg4" type="commentary">Budgen crossed out this line and wrote “Done” over it.</note>
                <note xml:id="note5" target="#seg5" type="commentary">Sigfried Giedion's address in Zurich was 7 Doldertal.</note>
                <note xml:id="note6" target="#seg6" type="commentary">On the reverse side of the letter are penciled notes in Budgen's hand that have nothing to do with Léon's letter and JJ's suggestions for <name type="work">JJMU</name>.</note>
            </div>
      </body>
